# Operator and development template. Never commit real secrets.
# Production: explicit release + scripts/cluster.sh; see deploy/k3s/README.md.
# A local class does not start here: `colloq run` writes its own .env for the
# machine it is teaching on (KERNEL_BACKEND=docker and nothing production-only),
# and so do make dev, make up and every other make target that needs a .env.
# Copying this file into .env means asking for the production defaults below.
PORT=3000
PUBLIC_URL=http://localhost:3000

# Keep local access on 127.0.0.1; use make host for student HTTPS links.
# Under make up this restricts HOST publication, not the container's listener.
# Without BIND_ADDR the development server/Compose default is every interface.
# Production has separate fixed loopback ingress at 127.0.0.1:30080.
BIND_ADDR=127.0.0.1
TZ=Europe/Moscow

# Interface language before the owner chooses a server-wide setting in /admin.
UI_LANGUAGE=ru

# --- Execution boundary ------------------------------------------------
# Production uses broker. Docker is accepted only with NODE_ENV=development;
# make up/make run set that explicitly, and so does colloq run - which is why
# a local class gets its own .env instead of a copy of this file: a teacher
# must never have to read "broker" here and guess that it does not apply.
# Native development:
# NODE_ENV=development KERNEL_BACKEND=docker npm run dev
# Every backend isolates rooms; unavailable infrastructure fails execution.
# The test backend and JUPYTER_URL/JUPYTER_TOKEN fixtures require NODE_ENV=test.
KERNEL_BACKEND=broker
KERNEL_RUNTIME_URL=http://colloq-runtime:8787
# Production installer mounts these files. Do not paste credentials here.
KERNEL_RUNTIME_TOKEN_FILE=
KERNEL_CATALOG_FILE=/etc/colloq/catalog.json

# Native macOS cannot provide Linux /proc/self/fd filesystem access, and this
# line used to be the price of running there. It no longer is: every segment is
# opened with O_NOFOLLOW on every platform (server/src/secure-files.ts), so
# `colloq run` asks for nothing here - a teacher must not have to type the word
# UNSAFE into a file to hold a class. A local class is trusted ground: your
# class on your computer. Production stays Linux in a container, where the
# remaining non-Linux race window does not exist at all.
# COLLOQ_UNSAFE_DEV_FILES=1

# Signs participant and staff credentials. Empty generates a persistent key in
# DATA_DIR. Preserve the key on restore; setting a different value rotates it.
SESSION_SECRET=
# Test fixture only. No instance-wide Jupyter service is started in production
# or Docker development. Secret defaults remain empty, including after a manual
# cp .env.example .env. make up derives separate development room credentials.
JUPYTER_TOKEN=

# --- Docker development only -------------------------------------------
# Production room limits come from broker configuration/runtime catalog.
# Memory for one room's container. 2g is sized for a laptop, not for a seminar:
# resnet18 over a batch of 250 images at 224x224 needs close to 2g of activations
# alone, on top of torch, the CUDA context and any model already loaded, and the
# cgroup then kills python while Jupyter quietly starts a new kernel. A machine
# with 74 GB should give a deep-learning seminar 8g-16g. Per-environment override:
# KERNEL_MEM_<ENVIRONMENT>, the name upper-cased with non-alphanumerics as _ ,
# e.g. KERNEL_MEM_BASE_GPU=16g for the base-gpu image. Raising it on a running
# room needs no restart: docker update --memory=16g --memory-swap=16g colloq-room-<id>
# Умолчание без этой строки: 4g обычному окружению, 16g окружению с GPU.
KERNEL_MEM=4g
# KERNEL_MEM_BASE_GPU=16g
KERNEL_CPUS=2
KERNEL_SHM=1g
# Personal notebooks of students run in a SECOND container per class — one that
# never gets the class's GPU and has its own memory cgroup, so a greedy draft
# cannot pull the OOM killer onto the teacher's kernel. It holds one kernel per
# open personal notebook, which is why it needs its own two numbers: how many
# kernels may live in it at once, and how many processes it may hold (each
# ipykernel is about fifteen threads, so the room's 512 runs out around thirty).
KERNEL_OWN_MAX=40
KERNEL_OWN_PIDS=2048
# A personal notebook's kernel is stopped after this many idle minutes and its
# container goes with the last kernel in it: a class left open all day would
# otherwise hold one Python per draft anybody ever ran. 0 turns this off.
KERNEL_OWN_IDLE_MIN=30
KERNEL_ENV=base
# Explicit Docker device IDs; one room per device, no implicit GPU sharing.
# KERNEL_GPUS=
# make up determines the Docker socket group and daemon-visible workspace path.
# A host-native development server must leave WORKSPACE_HOST_DIR unset.
# DOCKER_GID=
# WORKSPACE_HOST_DIR=
# Competition submissions run in their own throwaway containers, one notebook
# and one metric per submission, and DATA_HOST_DIR is to them what
# WORKSPACE_HOST_DIR is to rooms: the competition directory as the Docker daemon
# sees it. Leave it unset on a host-native server; without it under make up a
# submission silently receives an empty directory instead of its data.
# DATA_HOST_DIR=
# Which way submissions are executed: broker (isolated k3s Pods), docker
# (local development containers), or test (a stand-in that executes no code).
# Unset follows KERNEL_BACKEND: production uses broker; local development uses
# docker; tests use the stand-in. test requires NODE_ENV=test, and production
# refuses docker. Broker readiness separately checks execution and package prep.
# COMPETITION_BACKEND=docker
# Local Docker room network only; broker jobs use Kubernetes NetworkPolicy.
# KERNEL_NETWORK=colloq
# Every room container runs hardened: uid 1000, no capabilities,
# no-new-privileges, no IPv6, a process ceiling, and no access to local
# addresses (LAN, router, this machine, cloud metadata); the internet stays open.
# Processes per room (a fork bomb stops here, not at the machine):
# KERNEL_PIDS=512
# Subnet of the colloq-rooms network the server creates when it runs on the
# host; change it only if your LAN or VPN already uses it:
# KERNEL_ROOM_SUBNET=10.213.0.0/22
# The block is installed as iptables rules by a short privileged helper
# container; if that is impossible, rooms refuse to start. For a trusted class
# that needs the local network (or a Docker that forbids the helper), lift it:
# COLLOQ_ROOM_NETWORK=open

# --- Oracle ------------------------------------------------------------
# Any configured OpenAI-compatible endpoint. Empty credentials remain empty.
AI_PROVIDER=openai
OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_BASE_URL=https://api.openai.com/v1
OPENAI_MODEL=gpt-4o-mini
AI_REASONING=false

# --- Teaching and files -------------------------------------------------
INSTITUTION=
ADMIN_EMAIL=you@your-university.edu
OPEN_SEMINAR_CREATION=false
MAX_UPLOAD_MB=50
# Application quota; arbitrary Python disk writes need separate host controls.
MAX_SESSION_MB=1024
# Memory one council attempt may spend on personal copies of the room's
# data. Above the budget a variable stays shared and the attempt is told so.
COUNCIL_COPY_MB=512
# Cap one council attempt's address space so a greedy attempt fails alone
# instead of the OOM killer taking the whole room's kernel. 0 turns it off.
COUNCIL_MEMORY_GUARD=1

# --- Publishing the application ----------------------------------------
# make host: quick Cloudflare tunnel, or a configured named tunnel/own relay.
# make host-direct HOST=seminar.example.edu: Caddy on a public Linux host.
# The proxy reaches production at 127.0.0.1:30080, not a room/broker port.
# Relay setup: make relay-setup WHERE=root@your-relay
# The relay is shared infrastructure; size and monitor it for your audience.
RELAY_DOMAIN=
RELAY_ADDR=
RELAY_PORT=7000
RELAY_TOKEN=
# Operator-only DNS credentials, never required inside the application Pod.
CF_TOKEN=
CF_ZONE=

# --- Optional Vast VM deployment ---------------------------------------
# See docs/deployment-vast.md. Nothing is rented by creating this file.
# make vast-up NAME=course RELEASE=/path/release.json HOST=course.example.edu
# requires explicit release/tooling, account/SSH checks and a price confirmation.
VAST_TOKEN=
# Supply a separate pull-only Docker registry config before a private-image rent.
VAST_REGISTRY_CONFIG=
# Explicitly attest that the credential is pull-only (scope cannot be checked
# offline), or declare that every release image is publicly pullable:
# VAST_REGISTRY_READ_ONLY=1
# VAST_PUBLIC_IMAGES=1
# VAST_LABEL=colloq
# VAST_SSH_KEY=~/.ssh/id_ed25519
# VAST_GPU=RTX 4090
# VAST_GPU_RAM=24
# VAST_MAX_PRICE=1.0
# VAST_DISK=60
# VAST_IMAGE=docker.io/vastai/kvm:ubuntu_cli_22.04-2025-11-21
# VM-only, on-demand. NVIDIA drivers must work; exact toolkit/plugin pins are in
# the release manifest. A real CUDA operation is required before GPU readiness.

# Google-таблица, куда make activity дописывает активность семинаров (id из ссылки).
# ACTIVITY_SHEET_ID=
